## PHASE 12: TECHNICAL MODERNIZATION AND PERFORMANCE

Review the current codebase and make meaningful improvements using modern, stable web development practices.

Preserve the existing technical foundation when appropriate.

Do not introduce unnecessary framework migrations or dependencies simply to appear more modern.

### Code quality

Improve:

- Component architecture.
- Reusability.
- State management.
- Responsive styling.
- Maintainability.
- Type safety where applicable.
- Error handling.
- Loading states.
- Accessibility.
- Asset organization.
- Unnecessary or duplicated code.

Remove obsolete or unused code only after verifying it is no longer required.

### Performance

Optimize:

- Initial loading experience.
- JavaScript bundle size.
- Image formats and dimensions.
- Responsive image delivery.
- Font loading.
- Caching.
- Rendering performance.
- Animation efficiency.
- Layout stability.

Aim for Google's recommended Core Web Vitals thresholds on real-world mobile and desktop visits:

- LCP: 2.5 seconds or less.
- INP: 200 milliseconds or less.
- CLS: 0.1 or less.

Treat these as performance goals, not results that can be claimed without measurement.

Do not lazy-load critical above-the-fold imagery when doing so harms initial rendering.

### Accessibility

Target WCAG 2.2 Level AA practices wherever applicable.

Ensure:

- Keyboard-accessible navigation.
- Visible focus indicators.
- Sufficient contrast.
- Appropriate focus management.
- Accessible forms.
- Semantic HTML.
- Proper labels.
- Understandable validation errors.
- Support for reduced motion.
- Readable typography.
- Suitable interactive target sizes.

### Security and reliability

Preserve security-sensitive integrations.

Never expose API keys or private credentials in frontend code.

Do not change payment processing, authentication, email delivery, customer information handling, or backend data models without understanding the existing implementation.

Do not introduce a database or authentication requirement unless the business genuinely needs one.

Avoid unnecessary infrastructure, complexity, and ongoing costs.

